# Horo Extension SDK @HORO_EXTENSION_SDK_VERSION@

This self-contained package is the public C ABI surface for native Horo
extensions. It has no compile-time engine or third-party dependencies and does
not require an engine source checkout.

Point `HoroEngineExtensionSdk_DIR` at
`lib/cmake/HoroEngineExtensionSdk`, then consume the header-only target:

```cmake
find_package(HoroEngineExtensionSdk @HORO_EXTENSION_SDK_VERSION@ CONFIG REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(my_extension PRIVATE HoroEngine::ExtensionSdk)
```

Create a portable starter project with:

```text
python bin/horo-scaffold-extension.py --shape backend --id com.example.tools --name "Example Tools" --output example-tools
```

Supported shapes are `gui`, `backend`, `script`, and `hybrid`. Generated
projects contain their manifest, C ABI entry modules, contract tests, and CPack
ZIP packaging configuration.

The SDK supports `horo-extension` host ABI versions from
@HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_MAJOR_VERSION@.@HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_MIN_HOST_MINOR@
(inclusive) to @HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_MAX_HOST_MAJOR_EXCLUSIVE@.0 (exclusive), and
publishes declarations through ABI
@HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_MAJOR_VERSION@.@HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_PUBLISHED_MINOR@.
Extensions declare the exact minimum host minor they require through
`horo_extension_query`; incompatible hosts reject the module before load.

See `share/horo/extension-sdk/extension-sdk.json` for machine-readable package
metadata. Validate a manifest without loading extension code with:

```text
bin/horo-extension-validate --json --schema-version 1 extension.json
```

The matching authoring schema is
`share/horo/extension-sdk/extension-manifest-v1.schema.json`. The validator is
the behavioral authority and reports stable codes plus exact JSON field paths.

Exercise a trusted native module's ABI negotiation, host callback ownership,
result-table shape, and unload path with:

```text
bin/horo-extension-conformance --json path/to/native-module
```

The harness reports stable stage-specific codes and exits nonzero for an
incompatible module. It loads native code in the harness process, so run it only
against trusted build output. Project scaffolding and distribution tools are
separate SDK deliverables.
